.TH CHTTPSVR_RESP_SET_HEADER 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_resp_set_header \- set a response header
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_resp_set_header(chttpsvr_resp *resp,
                                        const char *name,
                                        const char *value);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Sets or replaces a response header.  If a header with the same name
already exists (case-insensitive comparison per RFC 7230), the stored
value is replaced in-place and the function returns immediately.

Name casing: when a duplicate is detected the stored header name retains
the casing supplied by the FIRST call; the casing of subsequent calls is
ignored.  HTTP/1.1 header names are case-insensitive on the wire, so
this does not affect correctness, but callers who read back stored header
names directly should account for this behaviour.

All three arguments must be non-NULL; passing NULL for any of them
returns ccol_invalid_args without modifying the response.

name and value must not contain a CR or LF byte.  Both are written onto
the wire verbatim (as "name:value\r\n"), with no further escaping, so an
embedded CR/LF would let a caller that reflects request-controlled data
(a query parameter, a path parameter, an echoed request header) into a
response header inject arbitrary extra header lines, or split the
response into two, on behalf of whoever controls that data (HTTP
response splitting / CRLF injection).  Rejected with ccol_invalid_args
rather than silently stripped or truncated.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args if resp, name, or value is NULL, or if name or value
contains a CR or LF byte.
ccol_not_enough_memory if the underlying allocation fails.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_resp_set_status (3),
.BR chttpsvr_resp_write (3)
